  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Cloudy, damp, and cool through at least mid week.\n\nA line of thunderstorms crossing the Appalachians will run out\nof SBCAPE shortly, but will have some elevated (MUCAPE)\ninstability to work with as it progresses down the I-64\ncorridor. This will be enough for some downpours and lightning,\nwith any gusty wind potential likely limited to 45 mph or less\nat the higher elevations. Some repeat thunderstorm activity is\npossible where instability lingers near the mountains into\ntonight which could result in an isolated flood threat.\nConfidence was too low for a watch as of mid afternoon, but will\nmonitor as the evening progresses.\n\nOtherwise, high pressure already in place to our north across \nsouthern Quebec will rapidly strengthen to near 1040 hPa by \nTuesday night. As this occurs, northeasterly flow will \nstrengthen and the CAD wedge in place to the east of the \nAppalachians will strengthen. Large scale forcing for ascent \nwill also gradually increase from west to east tonight into \nTuesday as a shortwave trough and embedded upper low approach \nfrom the Ohio Valley. As large scale forcing for ascent \nincreases, the coverage of showers will increase substantially \nthrough tonight, and likely persist at times through Wednesday \nmorning. The precipitation may even resemble more of a \ncontinuous stratiform rain shield as opposed to showers as the\nCAD strengthens by later tonight.\n\nThe flow pattern across the CONUS over the next couple days will\nbe extremely complex, with a split flow pattern and a closed \nupper-level ridge building to the north of the upper low over \nthe Ohio Valley. So while the big picture of the forecast \ntonight through Wednesday has high confidence (low clouds, cool,\nand rainy), there is still some forecast uncertainty regarding \nthe finer scale details. A few model solutions attempt to \nproduce several inches of rainfall over localized areas as we \nbecome positioned in the right entrance region of a strong jet \nstreak developing off to our north and east, but there is lots \nof model to model inconsistency as to if that will occur and \nwhere. As of now, the most likely scenario appears to be for \nmost of the region to receive 1-2 inches of rainfall spread out \nover a prolonged period time tonight through Wednesday morning.\nIf this were to occur, the rainfall would be beneficial in \nnature given the ongoing drought conditions. However, if the \nsolutions that produce isolated totals of 3-6 inches were to \nplay out, there could be localized issues with flooding. Even if\nthe solution with higher end totals plays out, instantaneous \nrainfall rates won't be overly high given the lack of \ninstability, so it would be more of a prolonged soaking rainfall\nleading to areal flooding, as opposed to thunderstorms \nproducing flash flooding. We'll continue to monitor this \npotential as we move forward.\n\nEither way, Tuesday and Wednesday will feature low clouds, \ngusty northeasterly winds, and well below normal temperatures. \nTemperatures likely hold in the 60s on Tuesday, and may even \nstruggle to get out of the 50s on Wednesday as the CAD wedge \nreaches its peak strength.\n\nBy Thursday, high pressure to the north nudges southward enough\nto push drier air into the region and shunt most rain chances\nsouth into the Carolinas. Cloud breaks likely develop, though\ntemperatures remain below normal.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry conditions return later this week and into\nthe weekend.\n\nSurface high pressure builds northwest of the forecast area \nbeginning on Friday as a surface low pressure tracks northward off \nthe East Coast. Primarily dry conditions are expected, although\na nearby low pressure system brings increased precipitation \nchances to the eastern portions of the forecast area.\n \nUncertainty remains on the track and timing of the low pressure \nsystem, but recent guidance has it tracking north towards the \nNortheast away from the Mid-Atlantic, possibly making a cyclonic\nloop near Long Island. The most likely impact from the nearby \nsystem will be increased winds due to the tight pressure \ngradient overhead. North/northwest winds blow around 15-20 mph \nFriday and Saturday before decreasing Sunday and into the workweek.\n\nIn addition to mainly dry conditions and gusty winds, \ntemperatures gradually warm each day. On Friday, highs will be \nin the 60s for most (50s mtns) with overnight lows dipping into \nthe 40s and 50s. By the end of the weekend, high temperatures \nwill be in the 70s for most (60s mtns) with most staying in the \n50s overnight.",
